          <figcaption class="lm__caveats">
            <p><strong>The bold arrow</strong> in each row runs from the deeper side: context and orientation from dididecks-ai in the developer layer, measurement, proof and repair from Instadeck in the product layer. <strong>The thin arrow</strong> runs back: tests that pin documents, and variants a human ranks.</p>
            <p><strong>Why “moderate” for our product layer.</strong> dididecks-ai has no runtime prompt, no model call, no evaluation and no tests in the product. By an automated pipeline’s standard it is thin. But its product is a coding-agent session with about 4,500 lines of skills, a design contract per deck, a review surface and an export path. A reader who counts only automation should read it as thin.</p>
            <p><strong>Two carriers, or one.</strong> On our side Claude Code, the skills and context-v serve both the developer and the deck generator. On hers the layers are separated by design, down to a test that keeps operator notes out of product context.</p>
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        <div class="stories">
          <aside class="prototype" aria-labelledby="dvi-story-memory">
            <h3 id="dvi-story-memory"><span class="pill pill--signal">Second story</span> She turned our memory into product</h3>
            <p>Instadeck adopted Lossless’s <code>context-v/</code> and <code>changelog/</code> on 2026-09-23 and removed them on 09-29 in <q>refactor: productize creative memory</q>. The useful part, persistent creative memory, became operation-scoped database records, and a test now forbids the folders. Her reason: Instant Deck <q>inherits the spirit of AI Labs’ context discipline, not its full local-agent infrastructure</q>. Ours makes memory cheap to grow and easy for an agent to read; hers makes it per customer, typed and safe to expose to strangers.</p>
          </aside>
          <aside class="prototype prototype--thread" aria-labelledby="dvi-story-skills">
            <h3 id="dvi-story-skills"><span class="pill pill--thread">Third story</span> Skills cross layers in opposite directions</h3>
            <p>We write <code>SKILL.md</code> files for the dev layer, and they also steer deck generation because the generator is Claude Code. She runs 19 <code>SKILL.md</code> files inside the product, loaded by her own registry, which caps size, matches names to folders and whitelists five tools. Same format, different runtime: hers can’t wander, ours can adapt to a request nobody planned for.</p>
          </aside>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 3 · Highlights ──────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="highlights" id="ch-highlights" aria-labelledby="dvi-highlights">
      <div class="container">
        <p class="eyebrow">ch · highlights</p>
        <h2 id="dvi-highlights">Highlights</h2>
        <div class="cards">
          <article class="bench-card">
            <span class="bench-card__label">convergence · product layer</span>
            <div class="bench-card__body">
              <h3>Markdown first, and a DESIGN.md per deck.</h3>
              <p>Both write the deck as Markdown before rendering it: per-slide files with typed frontmatter, plus a per-deck design contract (ours via <code>maintain-design-md</code>, hers as <code>06-DESIGN.md</code>). Both reason about the whole deck before any slide. She tried a simpler path in PR 40 and reversed it in PR 47.</p>
            </div>
          </article>
          <article class="bench-card">
            <span class="bench-card__label">instadeck goes deeper · verification</span>
            <div class="bench-card__body">
              <h3>Verification, in both layers.</h3>
              <p>120 backend test files, tests that pin the spec and the runtime prompt byte for byte, a frozen render corpus, a read-only live acceptance runner, and a written rule that “technically valid” is not success. dididecks-ai has no tests; its one workflow deploys the splash.</p>
            </div>
          </article>
          <article class="bench-card">
            <span class="bench-card__label">dididecks-ai goes deeper · context</span>
