    <!-- 5 · Loops per layer ─────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="loops" id="ch-loops" aria-labelledby="dvi-loops">
      <div class="container">
        <p class="eyebrow">ch · the loops</p>
        <h2 id="dvi-loops">The loops, per layer</h2>
        <p class="section-lede">Same four lanes in every diagram. The two in-product loops share a spine: source, brand, research, narrative, design contract, whole deck, per-slide files, render, export. They differ in who closes the loop.</p>

        <h3 class="loops__h">Developer loops</h3>
        <div class="loop-notes">
          <p><strong>Instadeck.</strong> A spec or dated decision, then a Codex brief (a prompt pack or a one-off), one branch per concern, contract tests and CI, a pull request squash-merged to main, gate, rollback and review records, then a Railway deploy with migrations first. When a direction doesn’t hold, the loop goes back to the spec to simplify or restore.</p>
          <p><strong>dididecks-ai.</strong> An exploration or spec in context-v, a plan per phase, Claude Code loading the layered CLAUDE.md files and skills, a browser walk with no automated tests, a changelog entry and sitemap status flip, a commit to development, then pointer bumps, a shell release and per-site deploys. 111 commits, 99 with a Claude co-author trailer, no PR merges.</p>
        </div>
        <figure class="dvi-figure">
          <figcaption class="dvi-figure__title">Instadeck · developer loop</figcaption>
          <div class="dvi-figure__svg dvi-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Instadeck's developer loop, scrollable" set:html={loopDevThemSvg} />
        </figure>
        <figure class="dvi-figure">
          <figcaption class="dvi-figure__title">dididecks-ai · developer loop</figcaption>
          <div class="dvi-figure__svg dvi-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="The dididecks-ai developer loop, scrollable" set:html={loopDevOursSvg} />
        </figure>

        <h3 class="loops__h">In-product loops: two deck generators</h3>
        <div class="loop-notes">
          <p><strong>Instadeck: the customer uploads, the pipeline generates.</strong> Deterministic extraction, source design review and BrandDNA, bounded research, an investor story and redesign plan, <code>06-DESIGN.md</code>, then <code>slides/sNN.md</code> and whole-deck HTML. Chromium proves the render. A failure is measured and handed to a repair skill; a pass publishes an immutable <code>DesignVersion</code>. The loop closes on a machine measurement.</p>
          <p><strong>dididecks-ai: the operator drives Claude Code.</strong> Source deck, brief and call notes; a brand import and <code>DESIGN.md</code>; people and companies ingested; a narrative per slide; then phase 1, the whole deck as one Scroll-UI page. A human ranks each slot in place. A weak slot gets a new variant; a strong one is recreated as a 16:9 Play-UI file and exported. The loop closes on a human verdict.</p>
        </div>
        <figure class="dvi-figure">
          <figcaption class="dvi-figure__title">Instadeck · in-product loop</figcaption>
          <div class="dvi-figure__svg dvi-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Instadeck's in-product loop, scrollable" set:html={loopProductThemSvg} />
        </figure>
        <figure class="dvi-figure">
          <figcaption class="dvi-figure__title">dididecks-ai · in-product loop</figcaption>
          <div class="dvi-figure__svg dvi-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="The dididecks-ai in-product loop, scrollable" set:html={loopProductOursSvg} />
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 6 · Shape against harness ───────────────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="shape" id="ch-shape" aria-labelledby="dvi-shape">
      <div class="container">
        <p class="eyebrow">ch · shape against harness</p>
        <h2 id="dvi-shape">Shape against harness</h2>
        <p class="section-lede">On her side, instructions converge on one file of record per surface, and tests keep code and documents from drifting apart. On ours, instructions are layered by tree level, and the per-client folders are both documentation and the generator’s working memory.</p>
        <figure class="dvi-figure">
          <div class="dvi-figure__svg dvi-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="One repo against a tree, scrollable" set:html={shapeSvg} />
        </figure>
        <div class="two-col">
          <p><strong>One repo, two apps, four services.</strong> Instadeck holds a SvelteKit <code>frontend/</code> and a FastAPI <code>backend/</code> with workers and a renderer, deployed as separate Railway services. README routes readers to <code>product/</code>; the product harness sits in the backend, behind a wall that keeps repo memory out.</p>
          <p><strong>A pseudomonorepo inside a tree.</strong> dididecks-ai is a pnpm workspace inside ai-labs, inside the anchor monorepo. It holds an Astro integration (the deck shell, v0.4.0), her SvelteKit shell, an export CLI, and seven client deck sites as submodules, each its own repo and Vercel deploy.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 6b · Stack against harness ──────────────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="stack" id="ch-stack" aria-labelledby="dvi-stack">
      <div class="container">
        <p class="eyebrow">ch · stack against harness</p>
        <h2 id="dvi-stack">The stacks answer different users</h2>
        <p class="section-lede">Instadeck is a multi-tenant web product where the author signs in. dididecks-ai is a set of per-client sites where the reader signs in and git is the content store. Each stack shapes what users can do alone, and what an agent has to hold in its head.</p>
        <div class="stack__scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Stack comparison table, scrollable">
          <table class="stack__table">
            <thead><tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col">dididecks-ai</th><th scope="col">Instadeck</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><th scope="row">Shape</th><td>pnpm workspace: Astro integration, SvelteKit shell, export CLI, shared types, seven client sites as submodules</td><td>One repo, two apps; API, worker, renderer and frontend as separate Railway services</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Front end</th><td>Client sites: Astro 6, Tailwind 4 on Vercel. The shell: an Astro 7 integration on a package registry. Her <code>apps/frontend</code>: SvelteKit 2 with an in-memory mock backend</td><td>SvelteKit 2, Svelte 5, <code>adapter-node</code>, zod; routes for sign-up, dashboard, decks, versions, export, shared decks</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Back end</th><td><strong>None as a service.</strong> Dev-only Astro routes (<code>/api/slide-rank</code>, <code>/api/slide-decompose</code>) write into the client site’s working tree</td><td>FastAPI, SQLAlchemy 2, Pydantic 2, two Alembic lineages, LangGraph and LangChain, PyMuPDF, Playwright, OpenTelemetry</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Data</th><td><strong>Git is the content store:</strong> TypeScript registries, Markdown with frontmatter, an audits JSON. Reader access and telemetry in <code>astro:db</code> (libSQL, Turso); a migration off it is planned</td><td>Postgres with pgvector; a job queue on Postgres; S3-compatible object storage</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Who signs in</th><td>The <strong>reader</strong>, per deck: access tiers, single-use links, OAuth, magic links. Authors are operators with repo access</td><td>The <strong>author</strong>: accounts, workspaces, an admin</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Deploy</th><td>Vercel per client site from a git push; GitHub Pages for the splash; shell versions published and bumped per consumer</td><td>Railway: pre-deploy runs both migration lineages, a product-ready health check, restart on failure; a written gate and rollback record</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Models</th><td>Claude through Claude Code, at author time. The spec plans an embedded Claude chat</td><td>OpenAI as the single product provider (reset instructions); earlier routing across four providers kept on an archive branch</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Export</th><td><code>deck-export</code>: Playwright drives a running site to @2x PNG, outlined SVG, vector-text PDF, PPTX and real Keynote</td><td>Render-proven HTML, publication of an exact <code>DesignVersion</code>, an export service</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <div class="stack__grid">
          <article class="stack__card stack__card--them">
            <h3>What Instadeck’s stack makes easier</h3>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>For users:</strong> anyone can sign up, upload a deck and come back to it. Results persist per account and per version; nobody needs a repo, a terminal or an operator.</li>
              <li><strong>For agents:</strong> typed boundaries everywhere (Pydantic <code>extra="forbid"</code>, JSON Schemas for model output, versioned contract names) give an agent contracts to read and tests to run. A migration history tells it what the data looks like.</li>
            </ul>
          </article>
          <article class="stack__card stack__card--them">
            <h3>What it makes harder</h3>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>For users:</strong> the product decides the pipeline. A user who wants a slide no stage contract anticipates waits for Smart Edit or Smart Deck.</li>
              <li><strong>For agents:</strong> four services, two migration lineages and a coordinated deploy order are a lot to hold at once, which is why the gate and rollback record exist. A 10,982-line generation service is expensive to load. Every change has to be safe for strangers’ data.</li>
            </ul>
          </article>
          <article class="stack__card stack__card--ours">
            <h3>What dididecks-ai’s stack makes easier</h3>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>For users:</strong> each deck is its own site, with its own brand, URL and access rules; a reader gets a link, not an account. Anything a designer can build in Astro can appear on a slide.</li>
              <li><strong>For agents:</strong> Astro components and Markdown are what models write well. The whole deck is plain files an agent can read, diff and revert, with no content migrations and no shared database to break.</li>
            </ul>
          </article>
          <article class="stack__card stack__card--ours">
            <h3>What it makes harder</h3>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>For users:</strong> nobody outside the team can make a deck alone. The spec says the managing partner <q>will not open a terminal</q>. Authoring needs an operator and a build.</li>
              <li><strong>For agents:</strong> seven consumers of one shell across Astro 6 and 7, pointers to bump, a package to version. And Play-UI, the surface that ships, is the one CLAUDE.md says Claude is <q>bad at</q>.</li>
            </ul>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 6c · Data, architecture, failure ────────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="tradeoffs" id="ch-tradeoffs" aria-labelledby="dvi-tradeoffs">
      <div class="container">
        <p class="eyebrow">ch · three trade-offs</p>
        <h2 id="dvi-tradeoffs">Data, architecture, failure</h2>
        <p class="section-lede">Three choices where each side fits the content it holds and the people it serves. Hers is multi-tenant: many users, private uploads, immutable versions with lineage. Ours is engagement-shaped: a handful of clients, each team iterating on a few decks for weeks.</p>

        <h3 class="tradeoff__title">Postgres records against files in git</h3>
        <div class="stack__scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Data comparison table, scrollable">
          <table class="stack__table">
            <thead><tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col">Files in git (dididecks-ai)</th><th scope="col">Postgres records (Instadeck)</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><th scope="row">Unit of content</th><td>A file: a slot in <code>slides.ts</code>, a narrative <code>NN-slug.md</code>, a slide component per variant</td><td>A row: deck, operation, working document (with a SHA-256 of its Markdown), <code>DesignVersion</code></td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">History</th><td>Git history; variants kept side by side; <code>/api/slide-decompose</code> refuses to overwrite</td><td>Explicit lineage records; publication never rewrites a prior version</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Isolation</th><td>One repo and one deploy per client</td><td>Workspaces in one database</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">What an agent sees</th><td>Everything in the client folder, including prior decisions</td><td>Whatever the API and tests expose; production data is out of reach by design</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Search</th><td>The Chroma corpus, outside the product, for the operator’s agent</td><td>pgvector in the same database; a bounded design-memory retrieval is specified</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Cost of change</th><td>Edit a file, commit, push</td><td>Migrations, deploy order, rollback planning</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <div class="stack__grid">
          <article class="stack__card stack__card--ours">
            <h3>Files make easier</h3>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Cheap iteration</strong>, and an agent that works with full context.</li>
              <li><strong>Every abandoned idea</strong> is one <code>git log</code> away.</li>
            </ul>
            <h3 class="stack__card-sub">Files make harder</h3>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Multi-tenancy, self-serve and access control</strong>, which is why our reader access already lives in a database and <code>context-v/models/</code> sketches a remote one.</li>
            </ul>
          </article>
          <article class="stack__card stack__card--them">
            <h3>Records make easier</h3>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Per-user persistence, sharing, quotas and audit</strong> come naturally.</li>
              <li><strong>Provenance:</strong> it is possible to prove what produced a given slide.</li>
            </ul>
            <h3 class="stack__card-sub">Records make harder</h3>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Ad hoc creative work:</strong> every new kind of artifact needs a schema, a migration and a test. Her own spec warns: <q>prove that the same requirement cannot be represented clearly in the working Markdown documents</q>.</li>
            </ul>
          </article>
        </div>

        <h3 class="tradeoff__title">One repo of services against a tree of client sites</h3>
        <p class="tradeoff__lede">Her repo is a modular monolith in source and a set of services at runtime. Ours is a tree: a shared shell, many thin client repos, conventions above.</p>
        <div class="stack__grid">
          <article class="stack__card stack__card--them">
            <h3>One repo makes easier</h3>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>One place to look,</strong> one spec of record, one CI, and one PR that shows everything a change touched.</li>
              <li><strong>Tests that reach across</strong> the front-end and back-end boundary: one test reads specs, routes and services.</li>
            </ul>
            <h3 class="stack__card-sub">One repo makes harder</h3>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Some files grow without a natural boundary</strong>, like the 10,982-line generation service.</li>
              <li><strong>Copied-in workflows stopped running</strong> without anyone noticing, because GitHub reads only the root workflows folder.</li>
            </ul>
          </article>
          <article class="stack__card stack__card--ours">
            <h3>A tree makes easier</h3>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Client work stays isolated:</strong> separate repos, access and deploys.</li>
              <li><strong>Generic parts are lifted into the shell</strong> only after a second client needs them.</li>
              <li><strong>An agent on one client</strong> loads only that client.</li>
            </ul>
            <h3 class="stack__card-sub">A tree makes harder</h3>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>A shell change reaches seven consumers</strong> through a package version and seven pointer bumps.</li>
              <li><strong>Instructions repeat at each level and drift.</strong></li>
              <li><strong>Cross-cutting checks have no single home</strong>, which is part of why there are none.</li>
            </ul>
          </article>
        </div>

        <h3 class="tradeoff__title">Failure isolation</h3>
        <div class="stack__grid">
          <article class="stack__card stack__card--them">
            <h3>Instadeck</h3>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>API, worker and renderer are separate services.</strong></li>
              <li><strong>Durable jobs survive restarts</strong>, a stale-job rescuer exists, and the spec requires terminal failures to be visible.</li>
              <li><strong>The shared trunk is Postgres:</strong> API, queue and lineage all depend on it.</li>
            </ul>
          </article>
          <article class="stack__card stack__card--ours">
            <h3>dididecks-ai</h3>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Each client site is its own deploy</strong>, so one client’s outage or bad build doesn’t touch another. Each consumer pins a shell version.</li>
              <li><strong>No server-side generator to fail.</strong> The failure mode is human: generation stops when no operator is at the keyboard, and a deck is only as current as the last session.</li>
              <li><strong>Reader access depends on libSQL</strong> per site.</li>
            </ul>
          </article>
        </div>
        <p class="tradeoff__verdict"><strong>For both sides:</strong> her trunk risk is one database; ours is one operator. Neither is wrong for its stage, and each side’s next step is the other’s strength. Her product could keep a “last good” static export per deck that serves when the API is down, as our Vercel sites do by construction. Ours could let a reader-facing deck regenerate a slide without an operator, as her pipeline does by construction.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

