              <span class="minibar"><span class="minibar__k">product</span><span class="meter"><span class={`meter__fill meter__fill--${b.key}`} style={`width:${(b.product / PRODUCT_ROWS) * 100}%`}></span></span></span>
            </div>
            {b.key === 'shared' && (
              <Fragment>
                <p class="bucket__summary">Few, because the two sides use different tools and stacks. The interest is in how each is used.</p>
                <h4>Developer layer</h4>
                <div class="prose">
                  <p><strong>READMEs</strong> on both sides route the reader elsewhere quickly: hers to <code>product/</code>, with a release-safety rule; ours to the specs, the splash, the client sites and the skills snapshot. Ours has drifted: it calls a mock-backed shell “active”.</p>
                  <p><strong>Agent scratch kept out of git.</strong> Both ignore the coding agent’s local folder. Hers also ignores <code>/docs</code> and <code>/scripts</code> by default, force-adding only the records she wants tracked, and labels Bitácora logs <q>not product or agent context</q>: an explicit line between notes and record that ours doesn’t draw.</p>
                  <p><strong>Deploy config.</strong> Hers carries policy in the config (<q>API startup must not race migration</q>); ours is small because static sites need little.</p>
                </div>
                <h4>In-product layer</h4>
                <div class="prose">
                  <p><strong>Agent Skills as domain know-how.</strong> Both use <code>SKILL.md</code> for craft rather than plumbing: hers for VC analysis and slide craft (<code>ic-critique</code>, <code>why-now</code>), ours for brand, design contract, ingest and imagery. Hers are short (271 lines for 19) and imperative; ours are long and explain why.</p>
                  <p><strong>Chromium as the judge of a slide.</strong> Hers proves every render before publication and measures geometry and asset visibility. Ours drives a running site to PNG, SVG, PDF, PPTX and Keynote, and the Play-UI rule (<q>No responsive CSS. No JS in the slide itself</q>) exists so export is faithful. Hers uses the browser to check; ours uses it to produce.</p>
                </div>
              </Fragment>
            )}
            {b.key === 'similar' && (
              <Fragment>
                <p class="bucket__summary">Same job, different form. Both layers are dominated by this bucket, which fits a shared lineage.</p>
                <h4>Developer layer</h4>
                <div class="prose">
                  <p><strong>Instruction files.</strong> Both built three levels: hers root, <code>backend/</code> and <code>frontend/</code>; ours anchor, ai-labs and dididecks-ai. Hers contradicted itself, so she removed it from main and wrote a shorter reset pinned by a test. Ours contradicts itself differently, with stale skill paths. Ours orients a cold agent in one read, including why; hers can’t silently drift.</p>
                  <p><strong>Specs.</strong> Her spec of record has a precedence order for authority, change control, a conformance ledger rating each requirement Implemented, Partial or Not proven, and a definition of done. Our main spec is a north-star document, still Draft. Ours keeps questions open while the product is being discovered across engagements; hers closes them so an agent can’t reopen them by accident. Hers costs earlier reasoning, which survives only on archive branches; ours costs an agent knowing what has been decided.</p>
                  <p><strong>Decision records.</strong> Her dated records open by saying what they do not authorize: <q>not approved for merge</q>, <q>not deployment authorization</q>. Ours record reasoning and loose ends. Her PR 39 recovery note is a candid account of a reversal.</p>
                  <p><strong>Living maps, runbooks, session memory.</strong> Our sitemap has a file per route with typed frontmatter; her runtime inventory says it <q>does not claim browser acceptance</q>. Our browser-drive blueprint is tree-wide; her live runbook is stricter: <q>Do not call the release complete unless the command exits zero.</q> Her Bitácora stays close to the operator and out of every agent’s context; our changelog is shared, searchable, and read by the deck agent.</p>
                  <p><strong>Briefs for a builder</strong>, a mirror image. We wrote data models so she “stops guessing at shapes”; she wrote a Codex pack to brief Codex on the dididecks model. These documents were the real interface between the two silos. <strong>Git:</strong> hers makes a change reviewable as a unit; ours makes the reasoning readable in <code>git log</code>. <strong>Coding agent:</strong> Codex and Claude Code; neither side’s artifacts are tied to its tool.</p>
                </div>
                <h4>In-product layer</h4>
                <div class="prose">
                  <p><strong>The Markdown document chain</strong>, the strongest convergence. Hers: <code>01-source.md</code> through <code>06-DESIGN.md</code>, then <code>slides/sNN.md</code>, each <q>creative source plus typed front matter</q>. Ours: narrative files with typed frontmatter and a 592-line <code>DESIGN.md</code> for one client. Hers adds earlier stages as named files, hashes and evidence IDs; ours keeps files across weeks of iteration and many variants. Her spec names a principle we never wrote down: <q>Markdown is not factual authority … Strong typing is required at safety/execution boundaries</q>.</p>
                  <p><strong>The generator.</strong> Hers is a service that runs unattended for any customer at any hour, in a large file that is hard to load. Ours can do anything Astro can render, and needs an operator for every change. <strong>The contract:</strong> her canonical prompt is fixed per release and customer-safe (default intent: <q>Make this deck much better.</q>); ours is a method an operator adapts per client, and it explains its reasons.</p>
                  <p><strong>Spec row to component.</strong> In June our exploration proposed a structured spec row compiled deterministically into a component. In August her prompt-system decision proposed a DeckSpec plus a deterministic compiler and left it unmounted. Both reached the same hybrid separately; neither has made it the main path.</p>
                  <p><strong>Judgement, knowledge, diligence.</strong> Her visual audit scored slides on eight dimensions, once; our rank pill records a four-state verdict per slot, continuously. Her knowledge is broad and pre-written (195 files); ours is narrower and grows from engagements. Her Due Diligence findings stay off the investor slides; our citation popover keeps sources one click away on the slide.</p>
                </div>
              </Fragment>
            )}
            {b.key === 'different' && (
              <Fragment>
                <p class="bucket__summary">On one side only, where the choice fits that side’s context, so nobody should “fix” it.</p>
                <h4>Developer layer</h4>
                <div class="prose">
                  <p><strong>Repo memory as product memory, refused</strong>, the deliberate counter-choice of the study. <em>Hers makes easier:</em> customer runs can’t be steered by operator notes; memory is per customer and typed; a new agent can’t mistake history for authority. <em>Harder:</em> development reasoning lives in PR titles, dated docs and a local log, so a cold agent has less to read. <em>Ours makes easier:</em> one place for both kinds of memory, cheap to add, readable by any agent, compounding across clients. <em>Harder:</em> an agent can treat an old exploration as current, and nothing separates a client’s confidential notes from the method.</p>
                  <p><strong>Hers:</strong> release paperwork for migrations and four services against customer data; patch transport through CI as a workaround for her agent environment; pivots kept as archive branches. <strong>Ours:</strong> pseudomonorepo rules, the shared skills library, the corpus and publishing, which exist because of seven client repos inside a 27-project tree.</p>
                </div>
                <h4>In-product layer</h4>
                <div class="prose">
                  <p><strong>Where the generator’s memory lives.</strong> Her knowledge loader excludes operator notes from <q>every product knowledge lane</q>; our generator reads the client folder, because it is the operator’s agent. <strong>Where generation will run</strong>, the deepest strategic difference: our spec bets on a local-first native app with embedded Claude chat, because the paying user won’t open a terminal and confidential material stays local; hers bets on a hosted web app. Ours keeps the agent’s flexibility and moves it closer to the user; hers keeps the user’s simplicity and constrains the agent.</p>
                </div>
              </Fragment>
            )}
            {b.key === 'missing' && (
              <Fragment>
                <p class="bucket__summary">On one side only, where the other could borrow it. Eight of nine run toward us, and all of those concern verification.</p>
                <h4>Developer layer (all on our side)</h4>
                <div class="prose">
                  <p><strong>CI that runs tests, and tests at all.</strong> The shell has shipped at least four versions to seven consumers with typecheck as the only automated check; her one workflow runs about 27 test files with <q>no paid calls</q>. <strong>Tests that pin documents:</strong> hers assert that the spec and genesis still say what they must, and that the spec’s prompt equals the runtime prompt; we rely on discipline and frontmatter. <strong>A review packet</strong>, headed <q>unfinished implementation. Do not merge or deploy this snapshot</q>: low priority, but it matters when someone else picks up unfinished work.</p>
                  <p><strong>Drift noted in dididecks-ai</strong> (not fixed here): CLAUDE.md lists two submodules where there are seven client sites and a corpus, and an old skills path; a README claim about a mock; architecture links to files that don’t exist; five skills in the snapshot differ from the anchor, and one isn’t linked anywhere a session can load it; a filemap last regenerated 2026-06-07.</p>
                </div>
                <h4>In-product layer</h4>
                <div class="prose">
                  <p><strong>On our side, the main lesson of this layer.</strong> A frozen, provider-free corpus of past passes and failure shapes, where we have 51 exported slides and nothing that checks a shell change against them. Acceptance runners with 11 conditions, where ours is a human walk-through. Measured failure, then a bounded repair, at exactly the Play-UI step CLAUDE.md calls hardest. Tests that keep the runtime prompt equal to the spec, where our equivalent, the skill pack, has drifted.</p>
                  <p><strong>On her side, a flow from ours.</strong> Variants side by side. Her genesis names the problem: <q>Sometimes the LLM produced an excellent redesign. Sometimes it produced generic layouts</q>. Her answer is constraint and measurement around one result per run; ours is optionality, every slot with several variants ranked by a human. Since her §21.2 acceptance is a human judgement, two or three treatments for the weakest slides would give it something to choose between. The cost is paid model calls, which her spec guards.</p>
                </div>
              </Fragment>
            )}
